The A10/A20 RTC driver leaves the 32 kHz low-speed oscillator (LOSC) on
the internal RC oscillator, also on boards that have a 32.768 kHz
crystal. On a Cubietruck the RC runs 3.2 % slow (about 31.7 kHz), so a
free-running RTC loses about 46 minutes a day; from the crystal it ran
at -28 ppm. LOSC also drives the CLK_OUT_A/B pins, which boards use as
the 32 kHz low-power clock of their WiFi/Bluetooth module. The vendor
3.4 kernel selects the crystal.

On the Cubietruck this matters beyond timekeeping: the AP6210 module
samples this clock when it is powered up, and if that happens while
LOSC runs from the RC oscillator, its Bluetooth radio returns no
inquiry or LE scan results until it is powered up again with the
crystal running. Selecting the crystal at RTC probe, before the
Bluetooth driver powers the module, avoids that.

This series lets a board describe its crystal and makes the driver
select it, similar to the A31 binding and driver:

  1/4 dt-bindings: optional clocks property (the crystal)
  2/4 rtc-sunxi: select the crystal when the clock is present
  3/4 sun7i-a20.dtsi: ext_osc32k fixed-clock node, unreferenced
  4/4 Cubietruck: hand ext_osc32k to the RTC

The switch is opt-in: only boards that reference ext_osc32k change
behaviour, in-tree only the Cubietruck after 4/4.

There is no manual for the LOSC_CTRL control bits. The driver writes the
value the vendor kernel leaves in the register, 0x9 (crystal, drive
strength 2), and only when the register does not already hold that
setting, so a warm reboot does not disturb a running crystal. The write
clears bit 14, which is set in the power-on value 0x6000. Bit 14 looks
related to an automatic fallback to the RC oscillator, but the hardware
also falls back with it clear: when the crystal stops or fails to start,
the RTC runs at the RC rate (one RTC second = 1.035 s) and LOSC_CTRL
bit 13 reads as set; it clears again when the crystal runs. A board
whose crystal does not run therefore keeps a working, RC-clocked RTC;
the binding still asks to describe the crystal only where it is fitted.

On this board the crystal stops when the SoC runs warm (about 43 C on
the SoC sensor under sustained CPU/GPU load; with a fan it kept running)
and restarts after cooling down. I take that as a property of this
board rather than of the series.

There is no Fixes: tag: LOSC was never switched in mainline, so this is
a missing feature rather than a regression.

Tested on a Cubietruck (A20, 2 GiB) with Armbian's 6.18.56: from a cold
power-on the driver selects the crystal (LOSC_CTRL 0x9) and the RTC
holds ext_osc32k. Rates were measured as LOSC is seen by the RTC,
against an NTP-synced system clock: RC -3.2 %, crystal -28 ppm over 15
minutes. On v7.3-rc7 the series builds with W=1, dt_binding_check of the
RTC binding passes, and dtbs_check (Cubietruck, Cubieboard2) reports
nothing about the RTC.

Investigation and patches were done with an AI assistant; I reviewed the
result and tested it on the hardware named above.

D.R.racer (4):
  dt-bindings: rtc: allwinner,sun4i-a10-rtc: add the external 32k
    crystal
  rtc: sunxi: run LOSC from the external 32k crystal when present
  ARM: dts: sun7i: add the external 32k crystal
  ARM: dts: sun7i: cubietruck: run the RTC from the 32k crystal

